# The C standard library, written in SemanticScript (D-153): ctype.h, string.h, stdlib.h, the
# basics of math.h, and a little of stdio.h, as glibc behaves in the C locale. `ffi.open("c")`
# binds to it in an executable on Windows and on Linux alike, and the loader resolves a mapped
# library's C imports against it. Its functions are `abi library` operations, reached only
# through the table the compiler builds (`machine.cLibrary`).
#
# This module is generated from tools/clibrary.dsl by tools/build.py.

clibrary is module
clibrary purpose "The C standard library written in SemanticScript: what ffi.open of c binds, and what a library the loader maps finds for its C imports (D-153)"
clibrary path clibrary
clibrary imports standard.machine
clibrary imports standard.system
clibrary imports standard.string
clibrary imports standard.math
clibrary imports standard.compare
clibrary imports standard.convert
clibrary imports standard.bits
clibrary imports standard.logic
clibrary imports standard.test
clibrary imports kernel
clibrary imports memory
clibrary imports output
clibrary imports heap
clibrary imports floats
clibrary imports wide
clibrary exports prepare
clibrary exports lookup
clibrary exports cStringLength
clibrary exports cLibraryName
clibrary exports byteAt
clibrary exports setErrno
clibrary exports lowInt32
clibrary exports boundedLength
clibrary exports errorText
clibrary exports errorMessage
clibrary exports memoryFind
clibrary exports charOf
clibrary exports isSpaceByte
clibrary exports digitOf
clibrary exports parseWhole
clibrary exports parseReal
clibrary exports runHandlers

memoryAccess is capability
memoryAccess purpose "Authority to read and write the memory C callers hand the library"
memoryAccess grants use machine.memory none

systemCalls is capability
systemCalls purpose "Authority to write to standard output and error, read the environment, take memory, and end the process"
systemCalls grants use system.calls none

# D-153: every function here is an `abi library "<name>"` operation: the C calling convention, its C name in the
# table the compiler builds (`machine.cLibrary`), never a linker symbol. Each behaves as glibc does in the C locale,
# on Windows too, so a program's foreign calls give the same results on both: `long` and `size_t` are 64 bits
# (LP64), ctype's functions give glibc's class bits, comparisons give the difference of the first differing bytes,
# and errno is one cell that `__errno_location` gives. Streams are the numbers 1 (stdout) and 2 (stderr), which the
# data symbols `stdout` and `stderr` hold. machine.state bytes 1536 to 1663 belong to this module: 1536 errno, 1544
# stdout's cell, 1552 stderr's, 1560 __stack_chk_guard, 1568 the block of ctype's tables (0 until first asked for),
# 1576 to 1599 the three cells `__ctype_b_loc` and its kin give, 1600 the block getenv last gave on Windows, 1608 a
# Float64 strtod reads into, 1616 the bits a hexadecimal strtod made, 1624 the stdin cell. Bytes 1792 to 1887 too: strerror's text and
# its digits.
errnoAt is constant
errnoAt purpose "Where errno is kept, in machine.state"
errnoAt type Int64
errnoAt value 1536

